IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `uc-core`

-- The Universal Credit Regulations 2013 (S.I. 2013/376), Schedules 3 and 4: how the text's periods of months are counted.
-- Source: ../../registers/source-bundle/uksi-2013-376.txt (plain-text rendering, not the official text). Target: the stable build 1.6.98. Layer 1.
--
-- The text speaks of "the first 6 months of the absence", of an absence that "exceeds, or is expected to exceed, 6 months", and of a
-- claimant who may not be treated as occupying two homes "for more than one month". It never says how a month is counted from a day.
-- Two helper rules fix one counting convention for all of them, so that every paragraph that uses a period of months uses the same
-- convention (fork U3-F8 in NOTES.md, assumed and not ruled):
--   * a day falls WITHIN THE FIRST N MONTHS from a start day if it is on or after the start day and before the day N months after it
--     (the day N months after 1 January is 1 July for 6 months; `core — the date reached by adding a time limit to a date`);
--   * a period that ends on a last day EXCEEDS N months if the last day is on or after the day N months after the start day.
-- So a period that begins on 1 January and ends on 30 June has lasted exactly 6 months and does not exceed 6 months; one that ends on 1 July does.
-- Both rules use the keel's date rule, which moves the day of the month back to the last day of a shorter month.

-- src:2242 | (2) In the case of a child or qualifying young person, the circumstances specified in this sub-paragraph are that the relevant determination relates to any time—
-- src:2243 | (a) during the first 6 months of the absence of a child or qualifying young person for whom the renter is treated as not being responsible in accordance with regulation 4(6)(a) (child or qualifying young person looked after by local authority) where, immediately before the local authority started looking after them, the child or qualifying young person was included in the renter's extended benefit unit and the renter's award included the housing costs element;
-- src:2254 | (5) The specified periods are—
-- src:2255 | (a) the first month of the non-dependant's temporary absence from Great Britain and, if the circumstances of the non-dependant are such as would be disregarded for the purposes of regulation 11(2) (death of a close relative), a further one month;
@ref Sch 4 para 11(2)(a) and (5)(a) (src:2242-2243) (src:2254-2255)
GIVEN day IS A DATE
      start IS A DATE
      `number of months` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`uc3 — a day falls within the first months after a start day` day start `number of months` MEANS
    (day AT LEAST start)
    AND (day LESS THAN (`core — the date reached by adding a time limit to a date` start (`Time limit` WITH quantity IS `number of months`, unit IS months)))

-- src:2125 | (1) Subject to sub-paragraphs (2) and (3), a claimant is to be treated as no longer occupying accommodation from which they are temporarily absent where the absence exceeds, or is expected to exceed, 6 months.
-- src:2126 | (2) Sub-paragraph (1) does not apply to a claimant who falls within paragraph 3.
-- src:2127 | (3) Where a claimant who falls within paragraph 6 is temporarily absent from the accommodation which they normally occupy as their home, the claimant is to be treated as no longer occupying that accommodation where the absence exceeds, or is expected to exceed, 12 months.
@ref Sch 3 para 9 (src:2125-2127)
GIVEN start IS A DATE
      end IS A DATE
      `number of months` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`uc3 — a period lasting to a last day exceeds a number of months` start end `number of months` MEANS
    end AT LEAST (`core — the date reached by adding a time limit to a date` start (`Time limit` WITH quantity IS `number of months`, unit IS months))
